N2  - Summary Timely and accurate situational information is essential for effective disaster risk management, both for emergency responders and for the public. Advances in remote sensing by satellites and drones, as well as the provision of ?crowdsensing? data from mobile phones and advances in ?big data analytics?, have improved Early Warning Systems (EWS) and assisted emergency response. New global telecom satellite constellations, notably Starlink, can now provide real-time communications before, during and after disaster events; emergency telecoms can also now be provided by portable internet hubs. The chapter also provides example Earth Observation (EO) systems and their technical specifications, which provide real-time monitoring. Telecommunications in disaster settings, therefore, need to be: accessible, portable, and fast. The Internet is the main source of real-time information globally. Even in times of normality, an effective telecom system is needed to provide satellite data for EWS, as well as to send warnings and related information to the public.
